  "notes": "A barroom boogie band: southern-rock guitars welded to a fast honky-tonk two-beat. `bpm` takes the entry's 69–160 span with `mode` at 148, inside what it calls \"the section's densest single tempo band\" — a 142–153 cluster across five singles. Keys are the stated A/E/G. This is also \"the most blues-derived harmonic vocabulary in the section\": twelve-bar frames, I–I7–IV, I–♭VII–IV and V7 throughout, so the twelve-bar skeleton I–IV–I–V–IV–I carries top weight at `maxChords` 6, `extensions.seventh` is the highest in the stick wing at 0.32 (that is where the stated I7 and V7 live, since a numeral here names a degree and never a quality), and `major_blues` takes a full third of the scale weight. The kit is the entry's one comparative claim — \"louder and wetter than the Chesnutt/Tippin kit — model a mid-sized room and a compressed snare\" — hence the loudest `velocityTiers` in the batch (accent 110–127), four percussion lanes at −3 dB and the loosest humanize of the radio wing. Drums otherwise follow \"straight 8th snare stream with a rock-sized backbeat, two-beat kick opening to quarters under choruses, hat closed on quarters, crash on chorus downbeats\". The bass switch is the stated energy lever: \"root–five two-beat with 8th-note pumping under choruses; the 8th-note switch at the chorus is the main energy lever\", modelled as a raised `anticipationProb` over the inherited boom_chick rather than by changing the rhythm the parent already names correctly. ⚠ INFERRED: velocity tiers, `percs` contents, humanize, and every melody and countermelody number. ⚠ The entry rates itself medium and says the harmony is \"inferred from the blues frame the tempos and titles imply [LOW-CONF]\" — that flag is repeated here, and the tempo readings are [LOW-CONF] as a class.",
